<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Dvina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Daugava</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Dvina, called the Daugava in Latvia, rises in the Valdai Hills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and Latvia to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a trade route linking the Baltic with Rus', settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krivichs">Krivichs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a> grew on its banks, and Riga at its mouth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-dvina.jpg" alt="Western Dvina" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Karlis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fluss-lv-D%C3%BCna.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
